cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "Donghyunhang (동현항): a single-player sailing and trading simulation game on a world map with hex sea battles, with starting values and rule numbers set through the esoteric language Donghyun-lang"
type: software
version: "0.20"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24106054"
date-released: "2026-11-15"
authors:
  - family-names: Kim
    given-names: Dong Hyun
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9883-0229"
    affiliation: "Inha University College of Medicine"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/Raspberry-Kim/donghyun-lab17"
url: "https://raspberry-kim.github.io/donghyun-lab17/"
abstract: >-
  Donghyunhang is a browser-based, noncommercial toy that follows only the
  broad frame of Uncharted Waters II (Daikoukai Jidai II; Koei, 1993): a
  captain leads a fleet across a 16th-century world map, buying low and
  selling high among forty-five ports that trade thirty-six goods in eight
  kinds. At sea the calendar advances one day per step while food and water
  run down; in port the player uses a market, a lodge, a shipyard, a supply
  menu, a guild with delivery contracts and a cargo hold sorted by kind.
  Meeting one of eight pirate bands, five of which keep to their own seas,
  offers five choices (fight, flee, pay a toll, pay in cargo, scare them
  off); fighting opens an 8x8 hex sea battle with cannon fire, boarding and
  a captains' duel, and captured ships can join the fleet or be sold. The captain is Podongi, the maker's Maltese dog. Starting values,
  rule numbers, the victory condition and time limit are set through a
  program written in the esoteric programming language Donghyun-lang, whose
  integer output is read as pairs of setting number and value by a shared
  adapter. The game runs on a small common engine (fixed 60-tick loop,
  calendar, square and hex grids with A* path finding, tile map renderer,
  scene stack) written in HTML5 Canvas and vanilla JavaScript and shared
  with Donghyunsam. The world map is derived from Natural Earth 110m land
  data (public domain) at one degree per tile, with four narrow straits
  opened by hand; prices, ships, rival fleets and all rule numbers are test
  values chosen for this work and were not checked against the original
  game. No graphic, sound, text, number or code of the original game is used
  or extracted. Uncharted Waters is a series of Koei Tecmo Games; this work
  is unaffiliated. Input is keyboard, mouse or touch, with a fullscreen
  mode. A voyage can be saved by hand to three browser slots or to a .dhsave
  file and loaded back, together with the applied settings, the calendar,
  the random-number state and the market prices. The whole game can be
  played by keyboard: numbered control buttons, arrow keys to pick ports in
  harbour or to steer at sea, and a hex cursor in sea battles.
  Sound effects are synthesized in the browser with the Web Audio API (no
  audio files) and can be switched off with a header button or the M key.
  A start card waits on the map until the player presses Sortie. The map
  shades the sea in three depths by distance from shore.
  The MIT license applies to the Donghyunhang code and documentation; the
  page fonts are under the SIL Open Font License. See LICENSE and
  CREDITS.md.
keywords:
  - sailing and trading game
  - simulation game
  - Uncharted Waters
  - hex grid
  - esoteric programming language
  - JavaScript
